% ======================================================================================== 1 Summary
\section{Summary}\label{sec:summary}
\begin{keybox}
\item Each tennis point changes who is likely to win, and on Polymarket the price \emph{is} that probability. Quotes
lag the point by about a second, so whoever sees it first can trade against stale prices.
\item The wallets that trade in the first 3\,s after a point (the \emph{fast tier}) earn this after fees, in and out
of sample; everyone else loses. Copying them at their prices has an out-of-sample Sharpe ratio of
<< V('v2.oos.sr') >>, but doubled fees wipe it out.
\item With computer vision and an assumed 1\,s video feed, our pre-registered simulation makes << V('sc.pre.oos.v1.usd') >> a day out of sample, about break-even. Our pipeline adds << V('e2e.ours') >>\,ms,
so an order is executable << V('e2e.total') >>\,ms after the point, inside the 3\,s bar.
\item Every blind test of a book we could actually trade failed, and so did a replay on real order books. We tried
<< V('var.total') >> variants, logged all << V('peeks.n') >> reads of held-out data and used no real money.
\end{keybox}

% ======================================================================================== 2 Hypothesis
\section{Economic hypothesis}\label{sec:hyp}
Given how often each player wins a point on serve, a Markov model of points, games and sets gives the exact chance that each player wins from any score \citep{klaassen2001}. A Polymarket price is
that chance in cents, so each point moves fair value by a known amount at a known moment. We call that amount the
\emph{swing} \(\Delta(k)\) of point \(k\): the chance of winning the match if point \(k\) is won, minus the chance if
it is lost. It averages << V('markov.lev') >> and reaches << V('calc.mk.bp.lev') >> on a big point (Appendix~A).

The news reaches traders in tiers: the court first, then video, then public score feeds
<< V('lat.espn') >>--<< V('lat.wta') >>\,s after the price has moved (Fig.~\ref{fig:race}). A maker whose quote still
rests when the point ends is picked off by whoever knows first: the adverse selection of \citet{glosten1985} and the
latency race \citet{budish2015} and \citet{aquilina2022} measured in stocks. In-play betting prices are known to react
late \citep{croxson2014,brown2012}. We add a wallet-level map of who is fast and a dollar value for
each second. Polymarket holds every \emph{taker} order (one that trades against a resting quote) for
<< V('venue.delay') >>\,s, giving makers time to cancel. The edge is shrinking: the fast tier grew from << V('ft.wallets.first') >> to << V('ft.wallets.last') >> wallets
while its edge fell << V('ft.slope') >>¢ a month (\(t = << V('ft.slope.t') >>\)).

\begin{hypbox}
\textbf{H6 (pre-registered).} We expect wallets that trade within 3\,s of a score move in Polymarket ATP, WTA and
Challenger match markets to earn a positive net \emph{markout} over 30\,s (profit at the price 30\,s later, after
fees), because the other side is a maker who has not yet cancelled a stale quote. The edge persists because physics
and data licences decide who learns first. If so, fast wallets picked on past months only stay positive every month
and out of sample; H6 fails if the markout is \(\leq 0\) in over a third of months, or out of sample. H1--H4 were
committed before any result (\texttt{<< V('prereg.hyp.commit') >>}, << V('prereg.hyp.time') >>); H6 was frozen before
we opened the held-out data.
\end{hypbox}

