% ======================================================================================== 4 Methodology
\section{Methodology}\label{sec:method}
\para{The fast tier and v2} We flag a score move when the 10\,s volume-weighted price moves at least
<< V('pol.det_c') >>¢ away from its prior 60\,s. Each month a wallet joins the fast tier if, in earlier months, it
traded at least << V('pol.q_prints') >> times in the 0--3\,s after a flag, in at least << V('pol.q_matches') >>
matches, with \(t > << V('pol.q_t') >>\). Our copy book, \emph{v2}, copies those wallets' trades, sizes each by its
resolution risk (the best of << V('sel.sizing.n') >> sizing rules, picked walk-forward), caps orders at << V('risk.order_usd') >> and exposure at << V('risk.netcap') >> shares net per match,
and holds to resolution. At the fast tier's own prices it measures what their speed is worth, not what we could
earn: copying the same trades 3\,s later loses (Fig.~\ref{fig:edge}a).

\para{Being fast ourselves} Our second strategy uses computer vision (CV): a model watches the video, calls the point as
early as it can and sends a taker order before the stale quote is cancelled. We have no tennis video, so we
simulate this trader on past points with an assumed feed latency (licensed feed not purchased); all other parameters
are measured. The order wins only if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:budget}
  V + \delta + D - \ell \;<\; L + R \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad V \;<\; L + R - D - \delta + \ell ,
\end{equation}
where \(V\) is the feed delay (how late the video reaches us), \(\delta\) our pipeline plus network time, \(D\) the
<< V('venue.delay') >>\,s taker hold, \(\ell\) how early the CV calls the point, \(L\) the \emph{stamp lag} (how long
after the ball lands the umpire's official point stamp is logged) and \(R\) when the book reprices relative to that
stamp. On << V('lat.n_points') >> points the book moved << V('lat.book_vs_stamp') >>\,s before the stamp. Nobody
publishes \(L\), so we show two readings: << V('cv.pre.lag') >>\,s, which we \emph{pre-registered} (wrote down before
seeing any result; \texttt{PREREG.md}, << V('t0.prereg.written') >> UTC), and << V('cv.cal.lag') >>\,s, a \emph{post
hoc} estimate from one day of data. The simulated trades are past points where the price later moved at least
<< V('pol.det_c') >>¢, so they are \emph{selected on outcomes, not ex ante}.

Our CV is real on table tennis. On << V('cv.eng.nmiss') >> held-out balls that went out (120\,fps), our live causal engine called
<< V('cv.eng.tp') >> early (median lead << V('cv.eng.lead') >>\,ms) and none wrongly, at << V('cv.eng.fps') >>\,fps on
one L4 GPU. On our own footage a video frame becomes a built order in << V('e2e.ours') >>\,ms; with a simulated 1\,s
feed, << V('e2e.net') >>\,ms of network and the 1\,s hold, the order can execute << V('e2e.total') >>\,ms after the
point ends, inside the organizers' 3\,s bar (Fig.~\ref{fig:f2t}).

