% ======================================================================================== 6 Risk
\section{Risk management}\label{sec:risk}
\para{Limits and de-risking} Each order is at most << V('risk.order_usd') >> and each match at most
<< V('risk.netcap') >> shares net, at prices from 5¢ to 95¢, so one match can lose at most << V('risk.maxloss') >> plus
fees; a << V('risk.daily_stop') >> daily loss ends the day, all in code. Set in advance, we stop if the
score feed is over << V('risk.feed_stale') >>\,s stale, vision over << V('risk.vision_stale') >>\,s stale, or latency
above its rolling 95th percentile; we halve size if the trailing 30-day edge falls below << V('pol.trail_half') >>¢,
and stop if it reaches zero, if drawdown hits << V('pol.dd_stop') >>, or if the venue changes its fee or hold.

\para{Model, concentration and tail risk} Wrong CV calls always fill, so an out call trades only at confidence
\(\geq\)0.95 and one confirmed false call halts the day. Phantom calls outside play come about
<< V('cv.phantom.hr') >> an hour; << V('cv.phantom.pre') >> a day would erase the pre-registered 1\,s OOS P\&L
(<< V('cv.phantom.cal') >> post hoc); their filter is coded but untested. The top five copied wallets
carry << V('conc.top5.is') >> of v2's P\&L in sample and << V('conc.top5.oos') >> out of sample. The UMA oracle settles
markets \citep{pmuma}, US persons may only close positions \citep{pmgeoblock}, and courtsiding breaches ticket terms
\citep{itf2026}. Factor risk is nil (largest factor
\(|t| = << V('fac.max_t') >>\); \(\alpha\) has \(t = << V('fac.alpha_t') >>\)) \citep{fama1993,carhart1997}.

