\section{Liquidity and capacity}\label{sec:liq}
v2 trades << V('v2.is.notional_day') >> a day, << V('v2.is.share_vol') >> of match volume; its OOS confidence interval
stays above zero only up to 1\(\times\) size (<< V('cap.1x.oos.capital') >> of capital), and 5\(\times\) loses. The
CV book has no capacity at the pre-registered lag (Sharpe << V('capcv.pre.sr0.is') >> IS, << V('capcv.pre.sr0.oos') >>
OOS even at its smallest size). Post hoc, taking at most half the stale depth at each point, its Sharpe halves at
<< V('capcv.half.oos') >> of capital OOS and << V('capcv.half.is') >> IS and peaks at << V('capcv.pmax.oos.day') >> a
day on << V('capcv.pmax.oos') >> OOS (Fig.~\ref{fig:capacity}). What binds is the few seconds after each point: the
book is << V('capcv.share_inplay') >> of in-play volume but << V('capcv.share_fast') >> of what the fast tier trades
0--3\,s after a point. Data is the other limit: the cheapest data stack (<< V('fin.fixed.low') >> a day) needs
<< V('capcv.low.oos') >>--<< V('capcv.low.is') >> of capital post hoc, and at 1\,s the book could pay at most
<< V('cv.cal.oos.maxlic') >> a month for data post hoc and << V('cv.pre.oos.maxlic') >> pre-registered, against
reported feed prices of << V('fin.feed.low') >>--<< V('fin.feed.high') >>. At today's fee COURTSIDE prices speed; it
is not yet a business.

% ======================================================================================== 8 Limitations
\section{Limitations and next steps}\label{sec:limits}
The stamp lag \(L\) is unmeasured and moves the break-even one for one (Eq.~\ref{eq:budget}). Our post hoc value
assumes humans at the court, but the 1\,s hold dominates: even for a 20\,ms bot the inferred lag is
<< V('cv.L_bot') >>\,s. One session with a licensed feed and a few test orders would measure \(L\) and show if held
orders are released on a whole-second clock; at 1\,s the feed pays for itself only if \(L \geq << V('cv.L_go') >>\)\,s,
so buying it is a business decision we have not made. Our CV accuracy comes from table tennis, tennis is simulated and
we never sent an order. A fee rise, a shorter hold or more fast wallets would break the strategy.\label{lastmain}

